WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.029
Apple has silently updated their 13 and 15”
MacBook Pros, so let’s talk about everything

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:08.650
that’s changed, along with what we’re
really disappointed about, as well as a couple

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:12.640
of reasons why it actually makes sense to
buy one of these new MacBook Pro’s right

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:13.640
now.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:18.140
First of all, my condolences to those who
recently bought a 2018 model just to find

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:20.720
out their MacBook is now inferior.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.390
Make sure to check your return date to see
if you can still return it.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:25.950
On the plus side, only a few things have changed.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:30.800
Both the 13 and 15” models have Apple’s
new 4th gen butterfly keyboard, showing that

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.780
the 3rd gen keyboard still had issues after
Apple added the silicon barrier to protect

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:36.800
it from debris.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.829
So for those who have been holding off on
the MacBook Pro because of the failing keyboard,

00:00:40.829 --> 00:00:43.550
it may now actually be fixed, once and for
all.

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:47.519
This almost seems like an act of desperation
by Apple to release an update with a fixed

00:00:47.519 --> 00:00:50.670
keyboard to get people to buy again, but who
knows.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:56.149
For the 13” MacBook Pro, the only change
is a speed increase on the $1800 models base

00:00:56.149 --> 00:01:03.819
processor, from 2.3GHz base clock to 2.4GHz,
and from 3.8GHz turbo boost to 4.1GHz, which

00:01:03.819 --> 00:01:07.240
isn’t too big of a change, but you’re
getting it for the same price as before.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:16.070
The configurable $300 i7 upgrade bumps you
up from 2.7GHz base to 2.8, and 4.5Ghz turbo

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:19.390
to 4.7, which we don’t think is worth the
cash.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:23.259
If you’re spending that much, just go for
the 15” model which includes a dedicated

00:01:23.259 --> 00:01:29.080
graphics card and a newly updated Intel 9th
gen 6-core processor that gets a much bigger

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:30.429
boost in performance.

00:01:30.429 --> 00:01:38.890
The base CPU goes from 2.2GHz base clock to
2.6, and turbo boost goes from 4.1Ghz to 4.5,

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:43.579
now that’s a huge difference considering
it’s priced at the same $2400, so for those

00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:46.079
who waited, you’re gonna get a lot more
value.

00:01:46.079 --> 00:01:51.320
You can now upgrade to the first ever 8-core
processor in any MacBook for $300 more, and

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:56.200
it can turbo boost up to a massive 5.0GHz,
which we think is worth it for anyone who

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:59.840
can actually use those extra cores, like for
RAW video editing.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:05.049
Now instead of doing that, you can pay just
$100 more for the $2800 model which comes

00:02:05.049 --> 00:02:11.910
with a slightly slower, but still 8-core processor,
the faster 560X graphics chip, and twice the

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:15.050
storage space, which we think is worth every
penny.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:18.210
To be completely honest, we don’t think
there will be much of a performance difference

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:23.720
between both of those 8-core processors, so
just going for the $2800 model is the best

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:24.720
value.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.720
We also highly recommend upgrading to the
Vega 20 graphics chip if you’re doing heavy

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:30.410
graphics work or gaming.

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:33.920
You can watch this video above to find out
why it’s well worth the price.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:39.080
Now you might be wondering why the 13” MacBook
Pro is still stuck on Intel’s 8th gen processors

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.160
instead of the new 9th gen processors.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:46.090
Well, take a look at Intel’s 9th gen product
page, it seems as though none of those processors

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:51.510
come with their premium Intel Iris Plus graphics,
which is a must for the 13” models since

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.520
they lack dedicated graphics chips.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.080
Other than the processor and keyboard upgrades,
literally nothing else has changed, which

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.830
is the most disappointing part.

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:04.380
Everyone’s been waiting for a complete redesign
with thinner bezels, and we, unfortunately,

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:05.510
have to wait even longer.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:10.110
And if Apple was planning a redesign for some
time this year, they wouldn’t update the

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:14.360
MacBook Pro right now, so it seems like we’ll
be waiting until next year.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:18.850
And there’s still been no word of the rumored
16” MacBook Pro, so there’s a chance that

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:22.320
model will include the new redesign and will
come next year.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.050
We think it makes more sense for Apple to
redesign the iMac first, starting with a new

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:30.350
iMac Pro potentially this year, and then redesign
the MacBook Pros afterward.

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:33.970
One thing that’s really disappointing is
that Apple’s base 13” MacBook Pro for

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:40.690
$1299 is still a 2017 model with an ancient
dual-core 7th gen processor and 2nd-generation

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:43.910
butterfly keyboard which is known for its
keyboard issues.

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:49.370
At that price point, many Windows laptop manufacturers
have moved onto 4-core processors, and some

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:53.790
even have dedicated graphics chips as well,
so if you’re planning to get a 13” MacBook

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:58.340
Pro, we honestly recommend just paying $1800
for the 2019 model.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:02.240
Otherwise, just look elsewhere or buy the
2018 MacBook Air.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.430
We’ve already ordered a 2019 15” MacBook
Pro with the new 8-core processor, so if you’re

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:11.570
not already subscribed, click that button
above and check out some of our MacBook videos

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:12.800
right there.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:19.940
Thanks for watching, and we’ll see you in
the next one!

